On 2008-12-27, Ray Dillinger <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
>
>
> There are a number of people who are motivated to code 
> roguelike games from scratch.  I'm one of them.  But, in 
> general, those who are motivated to code roguelike games 
> from scratch are not motivated to exactly copy existing 
> games.  
>
> Even if I were to code "Neohack", in which everything in 
> nethack were to be preserved unless I thought there was 
> a good reason not to, there'd still be a bunch of major 
> differences from nethack. 
>
> I'd like people's opinions and reactions to this laundry 
> list of game design choices.

Sure! Hopefully these comments are helpful to you.

> * First and biggest?  For personal and moral reasons, I'd 
>   refuse to put in items, monsters, and artifacts that are 
>   subject to existing copyrights without express permission 
>   from the copyright holders.  That eliminates Stormbringer, 
>   Greyswandir, Sting, Orcrist .... and many others.  I'd 
>   feel compelled to replace them with other items, but 
>   the other items would be *DIFFERENT*.

Obviously I can't comment on your personal and moral reasons, since
you didn't state them, and commenting on them wouldn't be very helpful
anyway. :) If it were me, I would get rid of some of them; I think
part of NetHack's flavor comes from borrowing things from other sources
(everyone loves the first time they see a TARDIS while hallucinating,
the quotes in the monster descriptions are great, &c.). On the other
hand, sometimes it feels like everything in the game is either borrowed
(Stormbringer) or horribly boring (Giantslayer). That's not the case,
but actually making some new items and plot dressing, as really for me
as a player or designer it would be just dressing, would be nice and a
great thing to see in a new version, whoever made it.

Some things are fantasy tropes on their own that I wouldn't take out
personally: Excalibur, say, but also a lot of the Tolkienesque stuff,
even though I expect that's still technically under copyright. I would
not feel a need to keep Sting and Orcrist in the game, though.

> * Magic markers probably wouldn't exist. They are almost
>   always detrimental to the game.  If they did exist, they'd 
>   be more class restricted to mages and priests than they 
>   are now and more restricted in their use (especially in 
>   spells not already known - the ability to write such 
>   might vanish entirely or be limited to once per character 
>   level). OTOH, scrolls and spellbooks would be more common 
>   and more available in shops. 

I think this is ultimately good but would require some rethinking of
certain parts of the game where most players assume they will ultimately
have access to a marker, and thus a goodly number of scrolls, by the
time they hit the Castle. Maybe it would be strictly good to remove it,
on the grounds that it would force people to learn and use strategies
other than "Scrolls of teleportation for gehennom, scrolls of enchant
foo for equipment, scrolls of gold detection for the planes."

I don't think shops are the equipment rebalancing solution, though,
at least not as they are currently implemented in NetHack. A character
who is kitted out can just walk all over a shopkeeper and take all the 
scrolls and books. I don't see that as very different from waiting until
you have a marker and then writing them... there's more randomness in 
what you get, I guess, but it still feels like a similar issue.

> * New puddings (and any other creature that reproduces 
>   in dungeon realtime) would not be generated with new 
>   equipment.  No very strong feelings about summoned 
>   intelligent creatures, however. 

Summoned intelligent creatures who show up next to you won't have time to
fish for equipment elsewhere. Intelligent creatures are usually (unless
magic-users) only interesting because they have items to use to try to
kill you. So I'd let them keep coming with stuff.

> * Wands of wishing would always have 2-3 charges, but 
>   would not recharge.  They'd be rarer.  Only one magic 
>   lamp per game would give a wish.  Wishes for artifacts 
>   would be likely to fail. 

Crawl's scrolls of acquirement (yeah, yeah, I know) let you choose a
class of item to wish for instead of a specific item. Some classes of
items have many more types than others, and so some types of acquirements
are much better than others. This is one of the many pitfalls in a system
of wishing where you don't write an item and either get it or not. But
I do think it would be awesome if you could successfully make wishes
more likely to give you a related but different item the more powerful
the thing you are wishing for is. For example, wishing for a tin opener
would always work, wishing for SDSM might instead get you a silver dragon
with increased but not 100% chance of dropping scales, wishing for the
Eyes of the Overworld will give you normal lenses 50% of the time. (With
some variance in that percentage based on luck, alignment, ...)

But that's what I'd do. Limiting the number seems like a good idea on
its own.

> * Artifact secondary powers (including resistances/
>   immunities in most artifacts) would be slightly randomized.  
>   Primary powers would be stable.  When you find a thing out 
>   of legend, you may find that the legend, while accurate 
>   in major points, has embellished a few details and gotten 
>   others wrong.

Yes!!!! NetHack needs randarts desperately to make the artifacts
interesting, in my opinion, and the Neohack you suggest would be 
well served by this. I like the idea of slight randomization rather
than total random generation, too; it seems more in keeping with the
NetHack flavor.

> * AC from prayer would be limited in the same way that 
>   divine protection is limited now. In fact the limitation
>   might apply to their sum.

That seems like a good step, but AC is broken enough right now (in my
view) that you really need to do more here. (I'm pretty sure Derek said
the same thing. :)

> * Unicorn Horns would be nerfed somehow.  There are 
>   dozens of possible ways.

Certainly! I am glad I don't have to pick one either. (I like giving
them charges, but I'm not convinced it's the best solution.)

> * Item curses cast by liches et al would mostly end when 
>   that particular lich gets destroyed.  

I like this flavor-wise but not effectiveness-wise. It takes some of the
teeth out of the endgame, as it stands now. If you want to mostly kick the
blessed/cursed items minigame, so that people no longer carry tons of holy
water to Astral, I'd hope you replace it with something else interesting.
Granted, since curses can be solved with "carry a bunch of holy water,"
the bar for interesting isn't that high. 

Derek's attempted to mitigate/solve this by making the destroy/repair
armor minigame part of Spork. From a design perspective I thought this
was a good idea, but when I played a couple of Spork games all the
way through I found it made me want to stab out my eyes. I'm not sure
if that means it's a good idea or not, but it'd be worth looking at,
playing through, and decided if you good-hated it or bad-hated it. For 
my part, I still can't decide.

> * Blessing inventory would be increasingly unlikely to 
>   work if you had more than 4 or 5 pieces of blessed 
>   inventory already.  

Not sure what the point of this is.

> * No poisoned item could ever be blessed.  No character 
>   carrying poison or unholy water (or certain artifacts) 
>   would be allowed to carry holy water at the same time. 

Allowed by whom? Just making it impossible doesn't seem sensical; I can
certainly see it being a god conduct issue. To me this and your other
"...would be allowed..." statement seem like great places to make NetHack
gods more interesting and more distinct from each other. If each one
had little conducts like this that you had to (unspoiled) learn from
vague catchphrases and screwing up and (spoiled) remember, making the
ideal kit for various characters _different_, that would be a big win
in my opinion.  Making every character have the same restrictions just
changes the ideal kit instead of diversifying it. Limiting wishes and
markers to make it harder to get the ideal kit helps, but to my mind
diversifying it is even better. But that's my goal, and not necessarily
yours.

> * No character carrying holy water (or certain other 
>   artifacts) would be allowed to carry unholy water or 
>   poison at the same time.

See above.

> * I'd probably reduce the fooproofing subgame in other 
>   ways too, and reduce the consequences of not bothering
>   with it. I think it's boring.

You probably will bad-hate the game mentioned above, then.

> * Artifact "Blasting" of cross-aligned wielders wouldn't 
>   be on pickup only.  It would happen at random intervals 
>   during play, maybe double or triple prayer timeout 
>   intervals. Quest artifacts of particular classes might 
>   sometimes blast wielders of other classes.

This seems reasonable enough but the blast being strictly in terms of
damage makes it largely uninteresting in the late game. "2d10 damage.
Yeah, I've got that." Why not making intelligent artifacts screw with
you in more interesting and irritating ways? "You attempt to engrave 
'Elbereth.' Magicbane writes 'Go to hell.' The mindflayer hits!"

> * #pray'ing while carrying a weapon or artifact whose 
>   alignment is opposed to your God's, or which is holy 
>   to some other God, might be a bit dangerous.  If 
>   you're lucky, your God will thank you for your 
>   "sacrifice" and take it from you.  If you're not so 
>   lucky, or you've been *using* the item extensively, 
>   then see the code for offended and angry Gods. 

I definitely like the "thank you for the sacrifice" angle. I think
there's a lot of potential here but there's only a skeleton here so far.
Which is fine! Thanks for sharing your thoughts and if you actually
decide to work on this, I hope you'll continue doing so.

